  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148271",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practices $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C∣{S}_{0})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C∣{S}_{2})=0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $48%$ of students in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.48.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.6%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C∣F)=0.026.\n$$\n\n- Since $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F∣C)=0.46.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis, which is represented as $P(C∣{S}_{1}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C∣{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C∣{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C∣{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{(0.026)(0.48)}{(0.25)(0.025)+P(C∣{S}_{1})(0.3)+(0.028)(0.45)} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C∣{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.46(0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})) & =0.0125 \\\\ 0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46} \\\\ 0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189 \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.3}(\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189) \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & ≈0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
